Firstly, let me explain the cause of this exception: When we use the `nextInt()` method from the Scanner class to read user input, it expects the next token to be an integer value. If the actual token does not represent an integer (for example, a string or a float), then the Scanner will throw an `InputMismatchException`.

To fix this issue, follow these steps:

1. Make sure that your program is reading the correct type of input. For instance, if you want to read an integer, make sure that the user enters an integer value.
2. Use the `hasNextInt()` method before calling `nextInt()` to check if there's an integer available for reading. This will prevent the InputMismatchException from being thrown if an invalid input is provided.

Here's an updated version of your code snippet:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter an integer value: ");

        // Check if there's an integer available for reading
        while (!scanner.hasNextInt()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
            scanner.next();
        }

        int userInput = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.println("You entered: " + userInput);

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

This code checks whether the next token is an integer before attempting to read it and handles cases where invalid input is entered. By doing so, it helps to maintain a good user experience and prevents the InputMismatchException from occurring.

